# Go Backend Development Standards

## Code Structure
- Follow Go project layout standards with `cmd/`, `internal/`, and `pkg/` directories
- Main application entry point is [cmd/api/main.go](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/cmd/api/main.go)
- All internal packages should be in [internal/](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/internal/) directory
- Use dependency injection pattern as shown in [internal/server/server.go](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/internal/server/server.go)

## Database
- Use SQLC for type-safe database queries (queries in [sqlc/queries/](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/sqlc/queries/))
- Database migrations are in [migrations/](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/migrations/) directory
- Database connection and service layer in [internal/database/](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/internal/database/)
- Generated SQLC code is in [internal/db/](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/internal/db/)

## API Development
- Use Gin framework for HTTP routing
- CORS configuration is set up for local development (localhost:5173, localhost:3000)
- Authentication via OAuth and JWT middleware in [internal/middleware/auth.go](mdc:tomlord.io-backend/internal/middleware/auth.go)
- Graceful shutdown pattern implemented in server

## Testing
- Use Testcontainers for integration tests with PostgreSQL
- Unit tests should follow Go conventions with `_test.go` suffix
- Run tests with `make test` command

## Build & Deploy
- Use Docker for containerization with multi-stage builds
- Environment variables are loaded via godotenv
- Use provided Makefile commands: `make build`, `make run`, `make docker-run`